        "items": [
          {
            "field": "Acceptance Criteria",
            "fieldtype": "custom",
            "from_string": "# Given the cart contains one or more items, when the drawer renders, then the header displays \"BAG • [item count]\" left-aligned with an X close icon right-aligned.\n# Given the cart contains no items, when the drawer renders, then the header displays \"YOUR BAG IS EMPTY\" left-aligned with an X close icon right-aligned.\n# Given a Shopper adds an item, removes an item, or moves an item to their wishlist while the drawer is open, when the cart updates, then the item count in the header updates dynamically without requiring a page reload.\n# Given the cart drawer is triggered, when the drawer renders, then it slides in from the right side of the screen — on desktop and tablet it occupies the right portion of the screen with the background page remaining visible but non-interactive behind an overlay; on mobile it renders full screen. The background page is non-interactive while the drawer is open.\n# Given the cart drawer is open, when the Shopper clicks the X close icon, then the drawer closes and the Shopper remains on the page they were previously viewing with no loss of page state.\n# Given the cart drawer is open, when the Shopper clicks or taps outside the drawer on the obscured background, then the drawer closes.\n# Given a Shopper closes the cart drawer via the X icon, Escape key, or clicking outside the drawer, when the drawer closes, then the Shopper remains on the page they were viewing with no loss of page state or scroll position.\n# Given the cart drawer is open and contains more items than fit on screen, when the Shopper scrolls within the drawer, then only the LHS drawer content scrolls (Cart Item, Zimmermann Benefits only) - see SFCC-270 and SFCC-316.\n# Given a Shopper opens the cart drawer on any device, when the drawer renders, then it displays correctly across desktop, tablet, and mobile breakpoints in line with Figma specifications.\n# Given the cart drawer is open, when the Shopper uses keyboard navigation, then focus is trapped within the drawer — the Shopper cannot tab to interactive elements behind the overlay.\n# Given the cart drawer is open, when the Shopper presses the Escape key, then the drawer closes and focus returns to the element that triggered the drawer.\n# Given the cart drawer renders, when it is read by a screen reader, then the drawer is announced with an appropriate ARIA role (e.g. dialog) and accessible label (e.g. \"Shopping bag\").\n# Given the X close icon renders in the drawer header, when read by a screen reader, then it is announced as a button with an accessible label (e.g. \"Close cart\").\n# Given the item count in the header updates dynamically, when the count changes, then the update is announced to screen reader users via an appropriate ARIA live region.\n\nMotion Design: The open and close animation for the cart drawer is defined in the Motion Design epic (see SFCC-86). The PWA Kit component must implement the animation as specified.",
            "to_string": "# Given the cart contains one or more items, when the drawer renders, then the header displays \"BAG • [item count]\" left-aligned with an X close icon right-aligned.\n# Given the cart contains no items, when the drawer renders, then the header displays \"YOUR BAG IS EMPTY\" left-aligned with an X close icon right-aligned.\n# Given a Shopper adds an item, removes an item, or moves an item to their wishlist while the drawer is open, when the cart updates, then the item count in the header updates dynamically without requiring a page reload.\n# Given the cart drawer is triggered, when the drawer renders, then it slides in from the right side of the screen — on desktop and tablet it occupies the right portion of the screen with the background page remaining visible but non-interactive behind an overlay; on mobile it renders full screen. The background page is non-interactive while the drawer is open.\n# Given the cart drawer is open, when the Shopper clicks the X close icon, then the drawer closes and the Shopper remains on the page they were previously viewing with no loss of page state.\n# Given the cart drawer is open, when the Shopper clicks or taps outside the drawer on the obscured background, then the drawer closes.\n# Given a Shopper closes the cart drawer via the X icon, Escape key, or clicking outside the drawer, when the drawer closes, then the Shopper remains on the page they were viewing with no loss of page state or scroll position.\n# Given a Shopper opens the cart drawer on any device, when the drawer renders, then it displays correctly across desktop, tablet, and mobile breakpoints in line with Figma specifications.",
            "from_id": "",
            "to_id": ""
          }
        ],
