        "items": [
          {
            "field": "Dependency",
            "fieldtype": "custom",
            "from_string": "@@Zimmermann: Open item — the decision on whether a real-time inventory check fires between cart and checkout entry is still pending. Once confirmed, the behaviour of the Checkout CTA when a stock check fails will need to be added to this story or covered in the inventory check story.",
            "to_string": "@Zimmermann: confirm whether guest and authenticated users are both taken directly to SFCC checkout entry without an intermediate login step - on Magento international users are directed to a separate login page first.\n@Zimmermann: confirm expected behaviour when a 100% discount promotion is applied and cart total is zero - should Checkout CTA remain enabled?\n@Accenture Dev: confirm how domestic vs international storefront detection works - is it storefront-based, IP-based, or address-based?\n@Accenture Dev: confirm whether a loading state is required on the Checkout CTA and duration.\nCross-epic dependency: SFCC-63 (Global-e Checkout Handoff) for international routing. SFCC-271 for quantity/inventory error disable states. SFCC-282 for OOS disable state. SFCC-277 for empty cart state.",
            "from_id": "",
            "to_id": ""
          },
          {
            "field": "Acceptance Criteria",
            "fieldtype": "custom",
            "from_string": "1. Given a Shopper views the cart with items and no quantity errors, when the page renders, then a black checkout button is displayed and enabled in the order summary panel.\n2. Given a Shopper clicks the enabled Checkout button, when the action is triggered, then they are navigated to the checkout entry page.\n3. Given a Shopper views the cart on mobile, when the page renders, then the Checkout button is full-width and positioned below the order summary.\n4. Given a line item in the cart has a quantity error — either exceeding available inventory or exceeding the maximum purchase limit (see [SFCC-271]) — when the error is present, then the Checkout button is disabled and greyed out.\n5. Given an out-of-stock item is present in the cart (see [SFCC-282]), when the error is present, then the Checkout button is disabled and greyed out.\n6. Given a Shopper resolves all quantity and availability errors in the cart, when the last error is resolved, then the Checkout button returns to its enabled state.",
            "to_string": "1. Given a Shopper views the cart with items and no errors, when the page renders, then the Checkout button is displayed and enabled.\n2. Given a Shopper on a domestic storefront clicks the enabled Checkout button, when the action is triggered, then they are navigated to the SFCC checkout entry page.\n3. Given a Shopper on an international storefront clicks the enabled Checkout button, when the action is triggered, then they are handed off to the Global-e checkout flow (see SFCC-63).\n4. Given a Shopper clicks the Checkout button regardless of authentication status, when the action is triggered, then they are taken directly to the SFCC checkout entry page without an intermediate login step. Open item: confirm with Zimmermann whether this matches intended behaviour for SFCC - on Magento international users are taken to a separate login page first.\n5. Given a line item in the cart has a quantity or inventory error, when the error is present, then the Checkout button is disabled and greyed out (see SFCC-271 ACs 4, 5, and 6).\n6. Given a fully out-of-stock item is present in the cart, when the error is present, then the Checkout button is disabled and greyed out (see SFCC-282).\n7. Given the cart is empty, when the page renders, then the Checkout button is hidden (see SFCC-277).\n8. Given a 100% discount promotion is applied and the cart total is zero, when the page renders, then the Checkout button remains enabled and the Shopper can proceed to checkout. Open item: confirm expected behaviour with Zimmermann.\n9. Given a Shopper resolves all errors in the cart, when the last error is resolved, then the Checkout button returns to its enabled state.\n10. Given a Shopper clicks the Checkout button, when the routing decision is being processed, then a loading state is displayed to prevent double-submission.\n11. Given a Shopper is on any device, when they view the cart, then the Checkout button renders and functions correctly across desktop, tablet, and mobile breakpoints in line with Figma specifications.",
            "from_id": "",
            "to_id": ""
          }
        ],
